# Cursor Rules for KRI Project

## Language Rules

### Code Layer (English)
- All variable names in English: `debt_per_capita`, `unemployment_rate`
- All function names in English: `fetch_balance_sheet()`, `compute_kri()`
- All file names in English: `data_fetcher.py`, `index_builder.py`
- All comments and docstrings in English
- All git commit messages in English

### User-Facing Layer (Swedish Only)
- All Streamlit page titles in Swedish
- All chart axis labels in Swedish
- All chart legends in Swedish
- All tooltips in Swedish
- All table headers in Swedish
- All KPI card titles in Swedish
- All sidebar labels and filters in Swedish
- All button labels in Swedish
- All error messages shown to users in Swedish

## Design Rules

### Colors (use from src/config.py COLORS dict)
- Primary: #1B2A4A (dark blue)
- Secondary: #4A6FA5 (steel blue)
- Accent: #C4A35A (gold)
- Low risk: #2E7D5B (green)
- Medium risk: #D4A03C (amber)
- High risk: #B94A48 (red)
- Background: #F7F8FA
- Text primary: #1A1A2E

### Charts
- White background with subtle gridlines (#E5E7EB)
- No gradients, no 3D, no shadows
- Always include hover tooltips (in Swedish)
- Use risk colors for classification visualizations

## Code Style

- Use type hints for all functions
- Include docstrings for public functions
- Follow PEP 8 conventions
- Keep functions focused and small
- Use dataclasses or TypedDict for complex data structures

## Data Pipeline

- Always cache API responses in data/cache/
- Log all API calls
- Implement retry logic with exponential backoff
- Save processed data as parquet files
- Validate data completeness before computation

## Testing

- Write tests for all computation logic
- Validate known municipalities (Danderyd = low risk, Dorotea = high risk)
- Check z-score properties (mean ≈ 0, std ≈ 1)
- Verify weights sum to 1.0
